cel flow =E2=80=94 attempts burned on conditions the sender cannot fix, whi=
ch is precisely what the environmental classification was built to prevent.=
</p>
<p class=3D"fld fix"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Suggested fix</span>Broaden t=
he environmental regex (reuse/extend isMessageBoxNetworkError: 'network err=
or', 'failed to retrieve messages', 'failed to send message', 'failed to fe=
tch', database locked/closed shapes) and classify 'Payment not found on ref=
resh' as environmental (or preserve and classify the ORIGINAL internalize e=
rror in acceptWithRetry instead of the relist error).</p>
<p class=3D"fld corr"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Verifier=E2=80=99s correctio=
n</span>Scenario (2) is folded into (1): the AuthFetch/SimplifiedFetchTrans=
port text 'Network error while sending authenticated request to =E2=80=A6' =
(SimplifiedFetchTransport.js:249) is caught per-host inside listMessages an=
d re-surfaced as the aggregate 'Failed to retrieve messages from any host',=
 so that exact string does not reach the classifier via the accept path =E2=
=80=94 same structural misclassification, one fewer distinct trigger. Mitig=
ations worth noting for the report: the NACKed row is not acked (onGiveUp d=
oc says 'Do not ack', handle.ts:273), so the money is still recoverable via=
 manual Retry, and sender-side resend requires a user tap (handleResendRequ=
ests.ts:173-176) =E2=80=94 the harm is a false 'uncreditible' NACK to the s=
ender plus a needs-attention row, not fund loss.</p>
