le.ts</code><code>packages/expo-wallet-toolbox/core/pay/creditInbox.ts</cod=
e></div>
</div></details>
<details class=3D"finding sev-medium" id=3D"inbox-force-mutex-concurrent-pa=
sses">
<summary><span class=3D"chip chip-medium">medium</span><span class=3D"ftitl=
e">Two concurrent force callers escape the creditInbox mutex and run overla=
pping passes</span><span class=3D"vtag v-confirmed">Confirmed</span></summa=
ry>
<div class=3D"fbody">
<p class=3D"fld"><span class=3D"fldlabel">When it happens</span>A silent po=
ll pass P is in flight. The user taps Retry on attention row A, then Retry =
on row B (busyId only disables the tapped row =E2=80=94 HandleReceive.tsx:2=
22 `isBusy=3D{busyId =3D=3D=3D id}` =E2=80=94 so B stays tappable, and pass=
es take seconds of network round-trips). Both force calls reach creditInbox=
Once, both observe inFlight=3DP, both `await inFlight.catch(...)` (creditIn=
box.ts:194); when P settles, both resume in sequence and each starts its ow=
n runCreditInbox and assigns inFlight (creditInbox.ts:195-196) without re-c=
hecking =E2=80=94 the two passes now run fully concurrently.</p>
<p class=3D"fld"><span class=3D"fldlabel">The defect</span>The mutex is che=
ck-then-set across an await with no re-validation, so N queued force caller=
s produce N concurrent passes. Each pass lists the whole box and attempts E=
VERY eligible payment, not just its forced id (runCreditInbox attempts all =
creditable rows below the ceiling). Ledger double-credit is prevented only =
by wallet-toolbox's re-internalize idempotency (verified: internalizeAction=
.js merge path sets payment.ignore for managed change), but the passes doub=
le-count failures =E2=80=94 the same failing payment gets +1 attempts in ea=
ch pass against the same loaded prior state, then saveInboxAttempts is last=
-writer-wins (creditInbox.ts:164), so attempts can jump straight to the cei=
ling and NACK early, or a concurrent discard's attempt-map cleanup is resur=
rected. One pass can also ack a message the other then relists as missing, =
producing the 'Payment not found on refresh' structural error (see inbox-st=
ructural-misclassification).</p>
<p class=3D"fld"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Impact</span>Premature give-up/NA=
CK on retryable payments, clobbered persisted attempt state, duplicated net=
work/SPV work; the very double-internalize window the module comment (credi=
tInbox.ts:4-6) claims the mutex closes.</p>
<p class=3D"fld fix"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Suggested fix</span>Loop inst=
ead of single-await: `while (inFlight) await inFlight.catch(()=3D&gt;{})` i=
mmediately before assigning, or chain force runs onto inFlight (`inFlight =
=3D (inFlight ?? Promise.resolve()).then(() =3D&gt; runCreditInbox(args))`)=
 so assignment happens synchronously with the check. Also disable all Retry=
 buttons while any forced pass is busy.</p>
<p class=3D"fld corr"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Verifier=E2=80=99s correctio=
n</span>The 'attempts can jump straight to the ceiling and NACK early' mech=
anism is wrong for truly concurrent passes: both load the SAME prior state =
(creditInbox.ts:144), so both write prior+1 and last-writer-wins UNDER-coun=
ts rather than jumps. What actually happens at the boundary is both passes =
crossing the ceiling together and sending duplicate NACKs (handle.ts:308-31=
5 fires onGiveUp in each). Early-NACK requires one pass persisting before t=
he other loads =E2=80=94 which is the benign sequential ordering.</p>
