x.ts</code><code>packages/expo-wallet-toolbox/ui/components/pay/HandleRecei=
ve.tsx</code></div>
</div></details>
<details class=3D"finding sev-medium" id=3D"inbox-hung-pass-stalls-monitor-=
forever">
<summary><span class=3D"chip chip-medium">medium</span><span class=3D"ftitl=
e">No timeout around the credit/drain passes: one hung await stalls the ent=
ire monitor and pins the mutex</span><span class=3D"vtag v-partial">Partial=
ly confirmed</span></summary>
<div class=3D"fbody">
<p class=3D"fld"><span class=3D"fldlabel">When it happens</span>An accept h=
angs =E2=80=94 a fetch that never settles on a flaky connection, or interna=
lizeAction blocked on a locked SQLite handle during a rebuild window.</p>
<p class=3D"fld"><span class=3D"fldlabel">The defect</span>creditInboxOnce =
keeps inFlight set until the promise settles (creditInbox.ts:196-201); Moni=
tor.runOnce awaits each runTask sequentially with no watchdog, so a single =
hung pass permanently stops TaskSendWaiting, TaskSendOffline, TaskNewHeader=
, TaskDrainOutbox, and every subsequent monitor cycle, while every HandleRe=
ceive poll and WalletCheckScreen credit step joins the hung promise and nev=
er resolves. TaskSendOffline had the same exposure before, but this change =
adds two more network-heavy tasks and, via the shared mutex, couples the re=
ceive screen to the background task's liveness.</p>
<p class=3D"fld"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Impact</span>One wedged request s=
ilently disables all background wallet maintenance and the receive screen's=
 inbox until app restart.</p>
<p class=3D"fld fix"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Suggested fix</span>Wrap the =
credit/drain callbacks in a generous timeout (e.g. Promise.race with 2-3 mi=
nutes) that rejects, clears inFlight (only if still pointing at the timed-o=
ut run), and lets the backoff reschedule.</p>
<p class=3D"fld corr"><span class=3D"fldlabel">Verifier=E2=80=99s correctio=
n</span>What does not hold is 'permanently': the scenario requires a promis=
e that literally never settles, and the cited failure modes generally do se=
ttle =E2=80=94 React Native fetch rides platform request timeouts (=E2=89=
=8860s on iOS NSURLSession, OkHttp defaults on Android) and a locked/closed=
 SQLite handle raises an error rather than parking forever in expo-sqlite. =
The realistic defect is multi-minute monitor stalls with no watchdog, an ex=
posure the pre-existing TaskSendOffline already had; this change widens it =
with two more network-heavy tasks and newly couples the receive screen's po=
ll to the background pass via the shared mutex.</p>
